<h2>1. Purpose</h2>
<p>
    These Terms of Service (the "Terms") govern your use of <strong>HouseFlow</strong>, an online
    service for tracking home maintenance: listing your houses and their devices, scheduling
    recurring maintenance, keeping a history of work done and its cost, and sharing that tracking
    with people of your choosing.
</p>
<p>
    HouseFlow is published by <strong>Rouss Consulting SRL</strong>, a private limited company
    incorporated under Belgian law, registered with the Belgian Crossroads Bank for Enterprises
    under number <strong>0805.984.579</strong> (VAT <strong>BE 0805 984 579</strong>). You can
    reach us at
    <a href="mailto:@LegalConstants.PrivacyContactEmail">@LegalConstants.PrivacyContactEmail</a>.
</p>
<p>
    By creating an account, you accept these Terms. If you do not accept them, you may not use the
    service.
</p>

<h2>2. Your account</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The service is restricted to <strong>adults</strong> (18 or over, or the age of majority in your country of residence).</li>
    <li>You create a single account, with a valid email address you control, and accurate information.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for keeping your password confidential and for any activity carried out from your account. Tell us without delay if you suspect unauthorised access.</li>
    <li>You can keep your information up to date under <strong>Settings → Profile</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Your obligations</h2>
<p>When using HouseFlow, you undertake to:</p>
<ul>
    <li>comply with the law and with third-party rights;</li>
    <li>not attempt to access data not intended for you, nor to circumvent access controls or technical limits of the service;</li>
    <li>not disrupt the service (abnormal load, abusive automation, intrusion attempts);</li>
    <li>enter in free-text fields (in particular maintenance notes) only information useful to the tracking, and <strong>never sensitive data</strong> (health, opinions, beliefs, sexual orientation, offences);</li>
    <li>invite to share a house only people who expect it, and record a service provider's contact details only as far as the tracking requires.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Sharing and roles</h2>
<p>
    You can invite other people to access a house. Each member is given a role that determines what
    they can see and do:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Owner</strong> — full control of the house, its devices, its members and their rights.</li>
    <li><strong>Collaborator (read and write)</strong> — can view and edit devices and maintenance records.</li>
    <li><strong>Collaborator (read only)</strong> — can view without editing.</li>
    <li><strong>Tenant</strong> — read access to day-to-day tracking: the house and its address, the list of
        devices and their details, upcoming maintenance and the history of completed maintenance (date, type,
        notes). They also see the list of the house's members, with their first name, last name and email address.
        They cannot create, edit or delete a device, a maintenance type or an existing maintenance record, and they
        cannot invite a member — the sole exception being the right to log maintenance described below.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Two of a tenant's rights are set member by member, and only the owner can change them:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Log maintenance</strong> — <strong>on by default</strong>. The tenant can record that maintenance
        was carried out, but cannot then edit or delete that record, even their own.</li>
    <li><strong>View costs</strong> — <strong>off by default</strong>. While it is off, the amount of each
        maintenance and the service provider's name are hidden from the tenant, and the total spent is shown to
        them as zero.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The owner can invite any role; a read-and-write collaborator can invite a tenant, without the
    owner having to step in. By inviting someone, you give them access to that house's data: check
    the role you grant. An owner can change those rights or remove a member at any time.
</p>
<p>
    A shared house's data also concerns the other members. If you delete your account while a
    collaborator remains, the house (<strong>including its address</strong>) and its history are
    transferred to them rather than destroyed; your account is no longer attached to it and the
    audit logs concerning you are anonymised. What you wrote in free-text fields (notes, providers)
    stays as it is. With no collaborator left, the house is deleted along with your account, even if
    tenants are attached to it.
</p>

<h2>5. Availability of the service</h2>
<p>
    HouseFlow is currently offered free of charge, as a <strong>launch (MVP)</strong> version. We do
    our best to keep it available and reliable, but we do not guarantee continuous availability or
    freedom from error: the service may evolve, be interrupted for maintenance, or have features
    changed or withdrawn. We will give you reasonable notice where possible.
</p>
<p>
    HouseFlow is an organisational tool: it replaces neither an inspection, nor a regulatory check,
    nor professional advice. The reminders and scores shown are indicative and computed from the
    information you enter alone. It remains your responsibility to have the mandatory checks on
    your equipment carried out.
</p>

